why api gateway when we have service registery or discovery server ?

An API Gateway and Service Discovery solve entirely different problems, even though they work closely together in a microservices architecture. [1, 2] 
Think of Service Discovery as an internal address book (it knows where services are dynamically running). Think of the API Gateway as a receptionist/security guard at the front door. Even if you have an address book, you still need someone to guard the entrance, verify identities, and manage incoming traffic. [1, 2] 
Here is exactly why an API Gateway is necessary even when Service Discovery is already in place:
## 1. External Entry Point vs. Internal Directory

* 
* Service Discovery is internal-facing: Tools like Consul, Eureka, or Kubernetes DNS keep track of the rapidly changing IP addresses and dynamic ports of your internal pods or containers. External clients (like mobile or web apps) should never interact directly with this dynamic backend ecosystem. [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] 
* API Gateway is external-facing: It provides a single, stable URL for external clients to hit. The Gateway handles the incoming public request, talks to the internal Service Discovery registry to locate a healthy instance, and forwards the request to the correct microservice. [1, 3, 4, 6] 
* 

## 2. Edge Security (Authentication and Authorization)
Service Discovery only provides locations; it does not secure them.

* 
* Without a gateway, every individual microservice would have to duplicate code to parse OAuth tokens, check user permissions, and handle SSL/TLS termination. [5, 6] 
* An API Gateway centralises security. It validates incoming JSON Web Tokens (JWTs) or API keys at the edge, rejecting unauthorized requests before they ever enter your private network. [6, 7, 8, 9] 
* 

## 3. Traffic Control and Rate Limiting
If a malicious user or a buggy client script floods your system with millions of requests, Service Discovery will happily point them to your services until they crash. [10] 

* 
* The API Gateway acts as a shield by implementing rate limiting, throttling, and request filtering at the edge. It limits requests per user or IP address to keep the system stable. [5, 9, 10] 
* 

## 4. Request Aggregation and Fan-out
Often, a single page on a mobile app needs data from multiple microservices (e.g., User Profile, Order History, and Recommendations). [5, 11] 

* 
* Without a gateway: The mobile app must query Service Discovery, get three different IPs, and make three separate network round-trips.
* With a gateway: The app makes one request to the Gateway. The Gateway orchestrates calls to all three internal services concurrently, aggregates their data, and sends back a single optimized response. [5, 11] 
* 

## 5. Protocol Translation and Transformation
External clients typically want to communicate via clean, web-friendly protocols like HTTP/REST or WebSockets. However, your internal microservices might use high-performance, compact protocols like gRPC or AMQP/Kafka for speed. [5, 8] 

* 
* Service Discovery doesn't modify data packets.
* An API Gateway can act as a translator, accepting a REST request from the public internet and converting it into an internal gRPC call. [5, 7] 
* 

------------------------------
## Direct Comparison

| Feature | Service Discovery (e.g., Eureka, Consul) | API Gateway (e.g., Kong, AWS Gateway) |
|---|---|---|
| Primary Job | Tracks the real-time location/IPs of dynamic services. | Serves as the single public entry point for clients. |
| Target Audience | Used by internal systems to find other systems. | Used by external web/mobile apps to talk to the backend. |
| Security | None (purely informational). | High (handles Auth, SSL, and DDoS mitigation). |
| Traffic Control | None. | Handles rate limiting, caching, and circuit breaking. |
| Data Handling | Doesn't touch payload or protocols. | Aggregates and transforms payload protocols. |

