## 1. Internal Structure of a Class Loader
The Java ClassLoader is a crucial part of the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) responsible for dynamically loading Java classes into memory during runtime. It does not load all classes at once; instead, it loads them when they are referenced by the application.
Its operations are governed by three core mechanisms:

* Delegation Model: When a class loader receives a request to load a class, it delegates the task to its parent class loader first. Only if the parent cannot find the class does the child attempt to load it. The hierarchy consists of:
1. Bootstrap Class Loader: The root loader (written in native C/C++), which loads core platform classes from java.base.
   2. Platform Class Loader: Loads non-core platform extension classes.
   3. Application Class Loader: Loads application-specific classes located on the environment classpath.
* Visibility Principle: A child class loader can see classes loaded by its parent class loader, but a parent loader cannot see classes loaded by its child.
* Uniqueness Property: A class loaded by a parent loader should not be re-loaded by a child loader, ensuring that duplicate classes are not created in memory.

