## 1. How do you implement API rate limiting in Spring Boot?
API rate limiting can be implemented at different levels depending on your architecture:

* API Gateway Level (Recommended for Microservices): You can use Spring Cloud Gateway integrated with Redis. By configuring the RequestRateLimiter gateway filter factory, Spring Cloud Gateway utilizes a Token Bucket algorithm backed by Redis to track and limit request counts globally across your services.
* Application/Service Level (Standalone Apps): You can use lightweight fault-tolerance libraries like Resilience4j. By adding the @RateLimiter annotation to your service methods, you can easily configure the maximum number of requests allowed during a specific time window via your application.yml file.
* Custom Filter / Interceptor: For simple monolithic use cases, you can use the Bucket4j library combined with a custom Servlet Filter or Spring Interceptor to intercept incoming HTTP requests and track tokens per client IP or API key.

