## 2. How do you deploy Spring Boot microservices using Docker?
Deploying a Spring Boot microservice with Docker typically follows a three-step process:

   1. Build the Artifact: Compile your application into a runnable JAR file using Maven or Gradle:
   
   mvn clean package -DskipTests
   
   2. Create a Dockerfile: Write a Dockerfile in the root of your project directory to define the container environment:
   
   FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jdk-jammyARG JAR_FILE=target/*.jarCOPY ${JAR_FILE} app.jarENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "/app.jar"]
   
   3. Build and Run the Container: Use Docker commands to build the image and spin up the container:
   
   docker build -t my-microservice:v1 .
   docker run -d -p 8080:8080 --name microservice-container my-microservice:v1
   
   
Note: In production environments with multiple microservices, Docker Compose or Kubernetes is used to coordinate, network, and manage all your containers together.
